.TH YAZC 1 "August 2026" "yazc 0.4.4" "General Commands Manual"
.SH NAME
yazc \- recover passwords and encryption keys from legacy ZIP archives
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B yazc
.RB [ \-h | \-\-help ]
.RB [ \-V | \-\-version ]
.br
.B yazc help
.br
.B yazc bruteforce
.RI [ options ]
.I archive
.br
.B yazc dictionary
.RI [ options ]
.I archive
.br
.B yazc plaintext
.RI [ options ]
.I plain-archive plain-entry cipher-archive cipher-entry
.br
.B yazc plaintext
.RI [ options ]
.B \-o
.I plain-file plain-start plain-end cipher-file cipher-start cipher-end cipher-begin
.br
.B yazc plaintext
.RI [ options ]
.B \-f
.I plain-file cipher-file
.br
.B yazc plaintext
.RI [ options ]
.B \-i
.I key0 key1 key2
.br
.B yazc plaintext
.RI [ options ]
.B \-p
.I password
.br
.B yazc info
.RI [ options ]
.I archive
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B yazc
operates on ZIP archives that use the legacy PKZIP stream cipher, commonly
known as ZipCrypto. It provides brute-force, dictionary, and known-plaintext
attacks, as well as a command for inspecting archive metadata.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B bruteforce
Generate and test passwords from a character set or a position-specific mask.
.TP
.B dictionary
Test passwords read from a file or standard input.
.TP
.B plaintext
Use known plaintext to recover the internal encryption-key representation,
or convert between passwords and internal representations.
.TP
.B info
Display the entries, offsets, sizes, and encryption headers in an archive.
.TP
.B help
Display global help and the list of commands.
.SH GLOBAL OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Display global help and exit.
.TP
.BR \-V ", " \-\-version
Display version and license information, then exit.
.SH BRUTE-FORCE OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-c ", " \-\-charset=\fICHARSET\fR
Use the characters in
.I CHARSET
to generate password candidates.
.TP
.BR \-i ", " \-\-initial=\fISTRING\fR
Begin the search at
.I STRING
instead of at the first generated password.
.TP
.BR \-l ", " \-\-length=\fINUM\fR
Set the maximum password length in character-set mode. The default is eight.
.TP
.BR \-a ", " \-\-alpha
Include lowercase ASCII letters (a-z) in the character set.
.TP
.BR \-A ", " \-\-alpha-caps
Include uppercase ASCII letters (A-Z) in the character set.
.TP
.BR \-n ", " \-\-numeric
Include decimal digits (0-9) in the character set.
.TP
.BR \-s ", " \-\-special
Include printable special ASCII characters, including space, in the character
set.
.TP
.BR \-m ", " \-\-mask=\fIMASK\fR
Use a position-specific password mask instead of character-set mode. Quote the
mask to prevent the shell from interpreting its metacharacters.
.TP
.BR \-k ", " \-\-mask-minlen=\fINUM\fR
Also test shorter prefixes of the mask, starting at length
.IR NUM .
The minimum cannot exceed the original number of mask positions.
.TP
.BR \-x ", " \-\-mask-maxlen=\fINUM\fR
Also test longer passwords, up to length
.IR NUM ,
by repeating the final mask position. The maximum cannot be shorter than the
original mask. Passwords cannot exceed 16 positions.
.TP
.BR \-t ", " \-\-threads=\fINUM|auto\fR
Use
.I NUM
worker threads. The value
.B auto
selects the number of online processors and is the default.
.TP
.BR \-S ", " \-\-stats
Print search configuration and runtime statistics.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Display help for the
.B bruteforce
command and exit.
.SS Mask syntax
Each mask position can be a literal character, a character list such as
.BR [abc] ,
an ascending lowercase, uppercase, or numeric range such as
.BR [a-z] ,
or one of the following placeholders:
.TP
.B ?l
Lowercase ASCII letters (a-z).
.TP
.B ?u
Uppercase ASCII letters (A-Z).
.TP
.B ?d
Decimal digits (0-9).
.TP
.B ?s
Printable special ASCII characters, including space.
.TP
.B ?a
All printable ASCII characters, from byte value 0x20 through 0x7e.
.TP
.B ?B
Upper-half byte values, from 0x80 through 0xff.
.TP
.B ?b
All non-NUL byte values, from 0x01 through 0xff.
.TP
.B ?h
Lowercase hexadecimal characters (a-f and 0-9).
.TP
.B ?H
Uppercase hexadecimal characters (A-F and 0-9).
.PP
Lists can combine literals and ranges. For example,
.B [a-f0-9_]
matches a lowercase hexadecimal character or an underscore. Use
.B \e?
for a literal question mark,
.B \e\e
for a literal backslash, or
.B \exNN
for the byte with hexadecimal value
.IR NN .
Unescaped whitespace is ignored; use
.B \ex20
for a position that must contain a space.
.PP
Without
.B \-k
or
.BR \-x ,
the generated passwords have exactly the same number of positions as the
mask. When
.B \-i
is used with a mask, the initial password must have the minimum generated
length and must match every corresponding mask position.
.SH DICTIONARY OPTIONS
The
.B dictionary
command expects one password per line. If no dictionary file is specified,
passwords are read from standard input.
.TP
.BR \-d ", " \-\-dictionary=\fIFILE\fR
Read passwords from
.I FILE
instead of standard input.
.TP
.BR \-S ", " \-\-stats
Print the selected input, archive name, and runtime statistics.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Display help for the
.B dictionary
command and exit.
.SH PLAINTEXT OPTIONS
The
.B plaintext
command uses a known vulnerability in the PKZIP stream cipher to recover the
internal representation of the encryption key. The attack requires at least
13 known bytes from the compressed plaintext stream before it was encrypted.
.PP
By default, plaintext and ciphertext are read from corresponding entries in
two ZIP archives. The
.B \-o
and
.B \-f
options provide two alternative mapping methods.
.TP
.BR \-o ", " \-\-offset
Map an inclusive range from
.I plain-file
to an equal-length range in
.IR cipher-file .
The final
.I cipher-begin
argument is the offset of the first ciphertext byte.
.TP
.BR \-f ", " \-\-file
Map bytes directly from
.I plain-file
to
.IR cipher-file .
The first 12 bytes of the ciphertext file are assumed to be the encryption
header. Bytes without a corresponding byte in the other file are ignored.
.TP
.BR \-i ", " \-\-password-from-internal-rep
Find a password from the three internal-representation values
.IR key0 ,
.IR key1 ,
and
.IR key2 .
See section 3.6 of the Biham and Kocher paper for more information.
.TP
.BR \-p ", " \-\-internal-rep-from-password
Calculate the internal representation of the positional
.I password
argument.
.TP
.BR \-t ", " \-\-threads=\fINUM|auto\fR
Use
.I NUM
worker threads. The value
.B auto
selects the number of online processors and is the default.
.TP
.BR \-S ", " \-\-stats
Print runtime statistics.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Display help for the
.B plaintext
command and exit.
.SH INFO OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Display help for the
.B info
command and exit.
.SH INFO OUTPUT
The
.B info
command prints one line per archive entry with the following fields:
.TP
.B INDEX
The zero-based index of the entry in the ZIP archive.
.TP
.B NAME
The entry name from the ZIP header.
.TP
.B OFFSETS
Three offsets useful with
.BR "plaintext --offset" :
the first byte of the encryption header, the first byte of the compressed
data, and the last byte of the compressed data. The encryption-header offset
is \-1 when the entry is not encrypted.
.TP
.B SIZE
The original, uncompressed size in bytes.
.TP
.B CSIZE
The compressed size in bytes, including the 12-byte encryption header.
.TP
.B ENCRYPTED HEADER
The 12-byte encrypted header, or all zeros when the entry is not encrypted.
.SH EXAMPLES
Try character-set passwords containing lowercase letters and digits, up to
eight characters, using four worker threads:
.PP
.nf
.RS
yazc bruteforce -a -n -l8 -t4 archive.zip
.RE
.fi
.PP
Try a literal pass- prefix followed by four decimal digits:
.PP
.nf
.RS
yazc bruteforce --mask='pass-?d?d?d?d' archive.zip
.RE
.fi
.PP
Read dictionary passwords from a file:
.PP
.nf
.RS
yazc dictionary -d words.dict archive.zip
.RE
.fi
.PP
Map bytes 100 through 650 of plain.bin to bytes 112 through 662 of
archive.zip. The first ciphertext byte is at offset 64:
.PP
.nf
.RS
yazc plaintext -o plain.bin 100 650 archive.zip 112 662 64
.RE
.fi
.PP
Map corresponding entries from an unencrypted and an encrypted ZIP archive:
.PP
.nf
.RS
yazc plaintext plain.zip file1.bin cipher.zip file2.bin
.RE
.fi
.PP
Map plaintext and ciphertext files directly:
.PP
.nf
.RS
yazc plaintext -f plaintextfile cipherfile
.RE
.fi
.PP
Find a password from an internal representation:
.PP
.nf
.RS
yazc plaintext -i 0x61852369 0x54cba4d5 0x1c5d5a2e
.RE
.fi
.PP
Calculate an internal representation from a password:
.PP
.nf
.RS
yazc plaintext -p pAssW0Rd
.RE
.fi
.PP
Inspect the contents of an archive:
.PP
.nf
.RS
yazc info archive.zip
.RE
.fi
.SH EXIT STATUS
.B yazc
returns zero when the requested operation succeeds and a nonzero value when
an error occurs or a password cannot be found.
.SH LIMITATIONS
Only the legacy PKZIP stream cipher is supported. ZIP archives that use AES or
other encryption methods are not supported.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR zipinfo (1)
